# fails the build when the two drift. It exists as a file because the image
# build reads it too — the last layer of the Dockerfile runs every line against
# the tk it just built, so an image whose tk predates one of these fails to
# build instead of booting a container that dies mid-run with "unknown command".
#
# Tracker commands only. The sandbox verbs and the branch write the scripts
# used to run through tk moved to ticfac with tick 46x — ticks became
# tracker-only — and the scripts call `ticfac …` for them. The
# ticfac in the image cannot lag the scripts the way tk could: the deploy
# cross-compiles it from the same tree it stages image/ from, so binary and
# scripts are the same commit by construction, and no gate is needed to say so.
